### Step 1: Problem Explanation
The problem is about finding "nice pairs" in an array. A nice pair is defined as a pair of indices (i, j) where i < j and the sum of the element at index i and the reverse of the element at index j is equal to the sum of the element at index j and the reverse of the element at index i.

### Step 2: Algorithm Overview
1. Reverse each element in the array.
2. Calculate the difference between the original number and its reverse for each element.
3. Use an ordered map to store the count of these differences.
4. Iterate through the array from right to left.
5. For each element, check if the difference already exists in the map.
6. If the difference exists, increment the count of nice pairs and update the result.
7. Update the count of the current difference in the map.
8. Return the final count of nice pairs.

### Step 3: Coding the Solution
```cpp
int reverse(int num) {
    int rev = 0;
    while (num) {
        rev = rev * 10 + num % 10;
        num /= 10;
    }
    return rev;
}

int countNicePairs(vector<int>& nums) {
    int n = nums.size();
    int res = 0;
    const int m = 1e9 + 7;
    map<int, int> mp;

    for (int i = n - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
        int difference = nums[i] - reverse(nums[i]);

        if (mp.count(difference)) {
            res = (res + mp[difference]) % m;
        }

        mp[difference]++;
    }

    return res;
}
```

