### Step 1: Problem Statement
The name of the problem is called "Closest Binary Tree Value Number Two." Given the root of a binary tree and a target value, as well as an integer k, the task is to return the k closest values in the binary search tree (BST) that are closest to the target. The solution should return the k closest values in any order.

### Step 2: Algorithm Explanation
1. Traverse the binary tree and calculate the distance of each node's value to the target value.
2. Use a max heap to keep track of the k closest values based on their distance from the target.
3. Implement a custom comparator to organize the elements in the heap based on their distance.
4. If the heap size exceeds k, remove the top element (farthest from the target).
5. Recursively traverse the left and right subtrees to add nodes to the heap.
6. Return the k closest values stored in the heap.

### Step 3: Implementation
```java
import java.util.PriorityQueue;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.ArrayList;

public class ClosestBinaryTreeValue {
    public List<Integer> closestKValues(TreeNode root, double target, int k) {
        PriorityQueue<Integer> pq = new PriorityQueue<>(new Comparator<Integer>() {
            @Override
            public int compare(Integer a, Integer b) {
                double distanceA = Math.abs(a - target);
                double distanceB = Math.abs(b - target);
                if (distanceA < distanceB) return -1;
                else if (distanceA > distanceB) return 1;
                else return 0;
            }
        });

        helper(root, pq, k);

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        while (!pq.isEmpty()) {
            result.add(pq.poll());
        }

        return result;
    }

    private void helper(TreeNode root, PriorityQueue<Integer> pq, int k) {
        if (root == null) return;

        pq.add(root.val);

        if (pq.size() > k) {
            pq.poll();
        }

        helper(root.left, pq, k);
        helper(root.right, pq, k);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Test your implementation here
    }
}
```

